{"items":[{"recordId":"teach-n0000799","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"A core interaction can produce a net that belongs to the larger class of proper nets, rather than remaining inside the subsystem’s original restricted class.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"A proper net is a net satisfying the structural conditions that make it a valid member of the broader net formalism.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000800","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The operator Ω records the order in which interactions occur within the subsystem.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"Ω also records the canonicalization operations that put the resulting net into its chosen standard form.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000801","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The inverse encoding φ−1 reads a net back.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The net is read back by φ−1 only after it is normal and canonical.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000805","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"Suppose a λL term reaches normal form after exactly n β-reductions.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"Its encoding in ΔL then reaches the corresponding normal form after exactly n net interactions, and this count does not depend on the order in which those interactions are performed.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000806","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The systems ΔA, ΔI, and ΔK extend the basic encoding with canonicalization rules for erasure structure.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"They also add canonicalization rules for sharing structure.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000807","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The nonlinear systems require canonicalization for the whole-algorithm claim.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"That claim also requires a global schedule for the whole algorithm.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000808","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The paper claims that the nonlinear systems satisfy Church–Rosser confluence.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"It also claims that these systems are optimal under the relevant computational cost model.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0000809","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"Church–Rosser confluence means that divergent reduction paths from the same starting object can be continued until they reach a common result.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false},{"role":"intuition","basis":"source","text":"Different choices during reduction may produce different intermediate forms, while the paths can still converge to the same eventual result.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]}]}
